-- title:
--   Principal divisors on a Weierstrass curve have degree zero
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field of characteristic $0$ and let $W$ be an affine Weierstrass curve over $F$, with function field $F(W) =$ `W.FunctionField`. The assertion is that $F(W)$, viewed as an extension of $F$, satisfies the class [`AlgebraicCurve.HasPrincipalDivisors`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L217): for every $f \in F(W)$ with $f \neq 0$ there is a divisor $D$, that is, a finitely supported function from the places of $F(W)/F$ to $\mathbb{Z}$, such that $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ for every place $v$, and such that $\deg D = 0$. Here a place of $F(W)/F$ is a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v \subseteq F(W)$ which contains the image of $F$ under the structure map, is not the whole of $F(W)$, and is a principal ideal ring; $\deg D$ is the integer $\sum_v D(v)\,\deg v$, obtained from $D$ by summing its values weighted by the residue degrees $\deg v$. Thus the statement packages two facts at once: a nonzero $f$ has $\operatorname{ord}_v(f) = 0$ for all but finitely many places (so that the prescribed function is indeed finitely supported), and the resulting divisor of $f$ has degree zero. No smoothness or nonsingularity hypothesis is imposed on $W$.
--
--   This is the degree-zero theorem for principal divisors, in the form needed for the function field of a plane Weierstrass model: $F(W)$ is a finite extension of the rational function field $F(x)$, generated by $y$ satisfying the monic Weierstrass quadratic, so the general result for finite extensions of $F(x)$ applies. It supplies the `HasPrincipalDivisors` instance used throughout the divisor-class-group layer, in particular in the analysis of modular polynomials and Vélu quotients.
